  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Scores on a certain standardized exam are normally distributed with a mean of $75$ and a standard deviation of $8.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected student scores less than $68?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ | $\\,0.2148\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected student scores less than $68$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the exam score. Then we have $X∼N(75,{8}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<68).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<68)=P(X\\le 68)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 68) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{68-75}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.88)=0.1894≈0.19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student scores less than $68$ is approximately $0.19.$",
